; a dome is an arch rotated 360° around its vertical axis.
; the dome is the most efficient large-span structure.
; pure compression throughout (no tension in a true catenary dome).
; spans vast spaces with minimal material — a thin shell.
; 
; structural behaviour:
; meridional forces: compression along the lines from apex to base (like arch ribs).
; hoop forces: in upper part compression (rings holding inward).
; in lower part tension (rings pulling outward against spreading).
; this is why masonry domes crack near the base (tension in stone = crack).
; tension ring at the base resolves the horizontal thrust (iron ring, concrete ring).
; 
; the Pantheon (Rome, 125 AD):
; 43.3m diameter concrete dome. 43.3m height from floor to oculus.
; the sphere fits exactly — a perfect geometric statement.
; unreinforced concrete: varying aggregate (pumice at top → lighter → less thrust).
; the oculus (9m hole at apex): reduces weight. provides light. completes the form.
; 
; geodesic dome (Buckminster Fuller):
; sphere approximated by triangular network.
; triangles are rigid. the whole structure acts in tension and compression.
; maximum volume per unit surface area — minimum material for enclosed space.
; all members approximately equal length — efficient fabrication.
; 
; pendentives and squinches:
; the problem: a circular dome on a square room.
; pendentive: spherical triangle of masonry filling the corner (Byzantine).
; squinch: small arch across the corner (Islamic, Romanesque).
; both transition from square base to circular drum — the geometry solved.

